How many records?

Database.QueryLocator → 50 million.

The reason it's this high is that a QueryLocator is a server-side cursor, not a materialized list. Salesforce streams records into execute() chunk by chunk; nothing ever holds the full set in memory. The 50M ceiling is the platform limit on what a single cursor will traverse.

Iterable<sObject> → 50,000.

Different mechanism entirely. You build and return the full collection inside start(), so it counts against the standard 50,000-SOQL-rows-per-transaction limit. There's no streaming.

That gap is the main reason to default to QueryLocator. If your selection criteria are too complex for a single SOQL statement, the better move is usually to query broadly with a QueryLocator and filter inside execute() — rather than dropping to Iterable and inheriting a 1,000× smaller ceiling.

*************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************

Default batch size

200 if you omit the second argument:

apex
Database.executeBatch(new MyBatch());        // scope = 200
Database.executeBatch(new MyBatch(), 50);    // scope = 50

Valid range is 1–2,000. Sizing guidance:

Scope	When
1–50	Callout per record, heavy trigger cascades, complex DML, high CPU per record
200	Straightforward field updates — the default is well-chosen for this
500–2,000	Light read-mostly work where per-chunk overhead dominates

The specific case worth remembering: if each record makes one callout, a scope of 100 hits the 100-callout limit exactly. Use 50 so you have room for retries or a second call.

Two details that surprise people:

If a chunk fails on a governor limit, the platform may automatically retry it with a reduced scope.
Very small scopes on huge jobs produce enormous chunk counts, and each chunk counts against your daily async execution limit.

***********************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************

When to use Database.Stateful

Default behavior: instance variables reset between every execute() call. Each chunk gets a fresh instance with only static final values intact. Database.Stateful serializes instance state and carries it across chunks.

apex
global class RevenueBatch implements Database.Batchable<sObject>, Database.Stateful {
    global Integer processed = 0;
    global Decimal total = 0;

    global void execute(Database.BatchableContext bc, List<Opportunity> scope) {
        for (Opportunity o : scope) {
            processed++;
            total += (o.Amount == null ? 0 : o.Amount);
        }
    }

    global void finish(Database.BatchableContext bc) {
        System.debug(processed + ' opps, ' + total);   // survived all chunks
    }
}

*************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************

Use it for:

Running counters or aggregation totals reported in finish()
A bounded error/failure list for a summary email
An ID or config value captured in start() that finish() needs
A flag deciding whether finish() should chain the next job

Avoid it when:

You don't actually need cross-chunk state — it adds serialization overhead on every chunk boundary and measurably slows the job
You'd accumulate large collections. A List<String> of errors across millions of records will hit the 12 MB heap. Cap the list, or write failures to a custom object and query them in finish()

Gotchas: only instance variables persist — statics still reset. Everything held must be serializable. And note that finish() can always query AsyncApexJob for JobItemsProcessed and NumberOfErrors, so if counts are all you need, you may not need Stateful at all.

