Callouts from batch

Yes, with the marker interface:

global class SyncBatch implements Database.Batchable<sObject>, 
                                  Database.AllowsCallouts {

    global Database.QueryLocator start(Database.BatchableContext bc) {
        return Database.getQueryLocator([
            SELECT Id, Name FROM Account WHERE Sync_Status__c = 'Pending'
        ]);
    }

    global void execute(Database.BatchableContext bc, List<Account> scope) {
        List<Account> toUpdate = new List<Account>();

        for (Account a : scope) {
            HttpRequest req = new HttpRequest();
            req.setEndpoint('callout:ERP/api/accounts');
            req.setMethod('POST');
            req.setTimeout(20000);
            req.setBody(JSON.serialize(new Map<String,Object>{'name' => a.Name}));

            try {
                HttpResponse res = new Http().send(req);
                a.Sync_Status__c = (res.getStatusCode() == 200) ? 'Success' : 'Failed';
            } catch (CalloutException e) {
                a.Sync_Status__c = 'Failed';
            }
            toUpdate.add(a);
        }

        Database.update(toUpdate, false);
    }

    global void finish(Database.BatchableContext bc) { }
}

Launched with a scope that respects the callout limit:

Database.executeBatch(new SyncBatch(), 50);

Points that matter:

Without Database.AllowsCallouts, a callout throws CalloutException — the interface is a pure marker, but it's mandatory.
100 callouts per execute(), and the limit is per chunk, not per job. This is why scope size and callouts are coupled.
There's also a 120-second cumulative callout timeout per transaction. Ten records at 20s each will blow it even though you're nowhere near 100 callouts. Set explicit setTimeout() values and size scope accordingly.
Callouts are allowed in start() and finish() too, subject to the same limits.
Wrap each callout in try/catch. An uncaught exception rolls back the entire chunk, losing every successful record in it.
You cannot call a @future method from batch — System.AsyncException. If you need follow-up async work, enqueue a Queueable from finish().

***********************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************

One testing note

In a test context, only one execute() invocation runs, regardless of scope size or how many records you inserted. Keep test data under a single chunk, or your assertions will silently pass against partial processing. To verify multi-chunk behavior — especially Database.Stateful accumulation — instantiate the class and call execute() directly with hand-built scopes.
